<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Long March</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Long March was the retreat of the Chinese Communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> from its bases in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1934–35, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s fifth encirclement campaign. The Central Red Army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> in October 1934 and marched some 10,000 kilometres through western China, losing most of its men, before reaching northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in October 1935. During the march, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zunyi">Zunyi</a> Conference of January 1935, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> rose to the leadership of the party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/long-march.jpg" alt="Long March" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guimard; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Long_March_1934-1935-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> was founded in 1921 and at first worked with the nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>. Chiang Kai-shek broke with it in April 1927, and the suppression of the party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and other cities, together with failed urban uprisings, drove many supporters to rural strongholds such as the Jiangxi Soviet, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> organised. By 1930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> had set up the Chinese Soviet Republic around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruijin">Ruijin</a>, in Jiangxi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a> adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a> and Bo Gu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> defeated four Nationalist attacks. The fifth campaign began in September 1933. Advised by the German Hans von Seeckt, Chiang's forces ringed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> with a fortified perimeter and besieged it. In July 1934 the party leadership forced Mao off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bo, Braun and Zhou organised the evacuation in complete secrecy, and even senior leaders learned of the movements only at the last moment. Some 16,000 troops stayed behind as a rear guard. By the time the marchers crossed the Xiang River on 1 December 1934, after heavy losses in breaking Nationalist blockades, only 36,000 men remained. At Mao's suggestion the army turned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, where Chiang had set up defences, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a>.</p><p>The Zunyi Conference met from 15 to 17 January 1935. Bo Gu blamed the defeats on the enemy's superior numbers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> blamed leadership decisions, himself among them; Zhang Wentian attacked Bo and Braun directly. The meeting reduced Soviet influence and strengthened Mao's position. Mao then avoided battle, crossing the Chishui River four times to escape encirclement before entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, and the army later crossed the Jinsha River and took the Luding Bridge on 29 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In June and July 1935 the First Army met the Fourth Army under Zhang Guotao at Maogong. Zhang, who commanded superior forces, challenged the leadership of Zhou and Mao, and the two armies went separate ways. Mao's army, attacked on the way by forces of the Hui Ma clique, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in October 1935 with about 8,000 survivors and joined the local Communists led by Liu Zhidan, Gao Gang and Xu Haidong. The Fourth Army was largely destroyed, and the Second Army under He Long completed its own march in October 1936.</p><p>The retreat cost the party most of its army but gave it the isolation to recover in the north, and the endurance of its survivors won it a good name among peasants. In 1935 Mao wrote that the Long March was a manifesto and a propaganda force. It marked the start of his rise to primacy and was featured heavily in his public image.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Long March” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kuomintang collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in Jiangxi in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the <b>Long March</b> of 1934–35 to northern Shaanxi, where Yan’an became its base, during which Mao Zedong rose to the leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiang turned on his Communist allies in the Shanghai massacre of April 1927 and fought them in a series of encirclement campaigns, forcing the Red Army onto the <b>Long March</b> in 1934–35.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Long March</b> the party was based in the Yan’an Soviet, where from November 1935 Mao produced most of the works later canonised as the Thought of Mao Zedong and won ideological independence from Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The fifth encirclement campaign, using blockhouses and blockade, broke the Jiangxi base, and in October 1934 the Red Army began the <b>Long March</b>, reaching northern Shaanxi in 1935.</blockquote><a href="/state/chinese-soviet-republic">Chinese Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
